WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.670
I’ve covered some pretty gruesome monsters before (shoutout to the manananggal) but the

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:10.300
Orcadian legend I put before you today ranks pretty high up there, even in my book.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.640
The Nuckelavee.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:17.680
Like a “hideous centaur” it is a fleshless
human torso atop an equally de-fleshed horse

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:18.680
body.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.440
That alone is terrifying.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:25.840
Then there are the tales of this monster about narrow escapes and widespread death and decay.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.590
The creature ravaged the Orkney Islands of the northern coast of Scotland, gleefully

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.890
killing humans, mangling livestock, and blighting crops.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:39.860
Declared “the most cruel and malignant of all uncannie beings that trouble mankind”

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:44.719
by an academic journal from the late 19th century, the Nuckelavee’s roots are entwined

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:50.480
with the history of Scotland itself, including its Nordic origins, its landscape of jagged

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.370
cliffs and raging sea, and the influence of the kelp industry.

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:55.850
Sound strange?

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.860
You have no idea.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.739
[Monstrum intro.]

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:08.359
Horses play a vital role in Scottish folklore but there is only one Nuckelavee.

00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:12.649
The monster gets its name in part from the word “neugle,”  which is

00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:16.470
any “water-spirit traditionally appearing
in the form of a horse.”

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:22.749
However, much more than a mere equine monstrosity “Nuckelavee” is a “spirit in flesh”

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:27.930
whose home is the ocean and whose name actually translates to “Devil of the Sea.”

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:33.659
The human torso boasts an oversized head that rolls side to side as if in danger of falling

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:36.209
off of the neck with a whale-like mouth.

00:01:36.209 --> 00:01:41.999
It has arms that almost reach the ground and is connected at the waist to a horse’s body

00:01:41.999 --> 00:01:45.919
with one red eye and “fin-like flippers
fixed to the legs.”

00:01:45.919 --> 00:01:51.969
No one knows exactly what shape it takes in the water, but on land the Nuckelavee assumes

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:58.700
the terrifying form of a deformed man riding a horse just “as terrible in aspect as himself.”

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.810
The man-horse chimaera appear as a singular, grotesque body.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:08.240
A late 19th-century text describes the fig-yer in gory detail: “the whole surface of the

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:14.190
monster appeared like raw and living flesh, from which the skin had been stripped.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.980
You could see the black blood flowing through his veins, and every movement of his muscles.”

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:25.110
Spoken of with “bated breath” and “unutterable horror,” this malevolent monster is hell

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:29.400
bent on wreaking plague and pestilence and has no redeeming qualities.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.950
The Nuckelavee is only active in winter since the mythical giver of life, Mither, or Mother,

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.599
of the Sea, keeps him restrained during the
summer months when she has control over the

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:42.400
sea and all its inhabitants.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.739
Every autumn the Teran, a hostile enemy winter spirit, battles Mither for control of the

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:48.939
ocean.

00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:53.580
Drained from the work of the summer months, she loses the battle and is forced to retreat,

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:58.590
leaving the Teran in charge, and the Nuckelavee free until the Mither’s strength is regained

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.379
the following Spring.

00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:05.219
Despite calling the sea his home, the Nuckelavee has an aversion to freshwater, which means

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:07.530
he never visits while it’s raining.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:13.489
Stories like this one from The Scottish antiquary, or, Northern notes and queries in 1891 speak

00:03:13.489 --> 00:03:18.599
to his terror of freshwater and the Nuckelavee’s grotesque appearance.

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:22.819
Legend has it that a man named Tammas was walking home along a road near the sea-shore

00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:28.260
on a moonless night, when he suddenly saw a huge, unearthly thing moving towards him

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.690
in the darkness.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:32.209
He realized the creature was “the dreaded Nuckelavee.”

00:03:32.209 --> 00:03:39.870
Tammas knew that he could not outrun the monster but he remembered the Nuckelavee could not  tolerate fresh water.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.680
So he hugs the edge of the loch on the opposite side of the path.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.340
In doing so, his feet splash fresh water onto the monster’s foreleg.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.849
The Nuckelavee shies away and Tammas runs off.

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:57.799
The creature pursues him, bellowing with a sound as deep and loud as the “roaring sea.”

00:03:57.799 --> 00:04:02.870
He could feel the creature try to grab him, but Tammas manages a narrow escape by jumping

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.749
across a small river.

00:04:04.749 --> 00:04:09.409
The Nuckelavee is enraged, unable to cross the fresh water.

00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:13.650
According to Scottish folklore, water in general was to be feared.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:19.030
The dangers of rushing water, pools of unknown depth, and the raging sea all pose a risk

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:21.440
to the living who aren’t cautious.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:28.340
But it is also a mode of salvation—water
does support life after all.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:33.240
In Scottish tradition water is a place of
collision—where water meets land, invaders

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:39.270
storm shores, and where ideologies may be
spread from one place to another through travel.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:44.120
To them, the shoreline was a boundary between
the supernatural and the natural.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:48.210
Perhaps that is why in Scottish lore water
also functions as a sanctuary.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:54.360
It is said that the shore below the high tide
line is a safe space from all manner of preternatural

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.729
creatures.

00:04:55.729 --> 00:05:00.490
Scottish folklore is full of water-horses,
but the Nuckelavee is unique in that it is

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.229
a solitary creature.

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:07.139
Other monsters like kelpies, and njuggles are said to be much more numerous.

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:13.039
Encounters with such creatures are always
dangerous—no benevolent water-horse exists.

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:18.229
But the Nuckelavee is even more threatening, as it is one of a very few sea monsters who

00:05:18.229 --> 00:05:20.250
come on land in Scottish folklore.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:24.759
So can we attempt to explain this horrible creature?

00:05:24.759 --> 00:05:29.770
The Orkney Islands close proximity to Norway meant its inhabitants experienced early and

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.120
lasting Nordic influence.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:36.390
And Nordic mythology is full of mythical horses.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:39.289
Take for example, Odin’s steed Sleipnir.

00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:44.410
With eight legs and teeth engraved with runes, he galloped through the air and across the

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:45.410
sea.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:49.470
Odin used Sleipnir to travel
to the world of the dead, Hel, which was ruled

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.410
over by a being of the same name.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:58.650
Hel the goddess was said to ride on a three-legged horse during times of pestilence and disease.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:02.699
One horse associated with the sea and another with disease and death?

00:06:02.699 --> 00:06:03.699
Sounds like the Nuckelavee.

00:06:03.699 --> 00:06:08.479
It’s possible that when Orkney was absorbed by Scotland in the 15th century, their culture

00:06:08.479 --> 00:06:13.220
appropriated these Nordic icons, made them more “monstrous” and thus was born the

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:15.250
Nuckelavee.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:21.490
Aside from running water there was one other thing that repelled the Nuckelavee—seaweed.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:26.080
Or rather it is said that “The burning of
sea-weed for kelp gave a terrible offence

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.860
to Nuckelavee, and filled him with diabolical
rage.”

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:35.599
While we now think of kelp as a type of seaweed,
through the 18th and 19th centuries kelp referred

00:06:35.599 --> 00:06:38.670
to the soda ash produced from burning seaweed.

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:43.620
This ash could be used to make glass and soap,
but the byproducts were also used as a food

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.990
source for people and livestock, as fertilizer,
and even in traditional medicine.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:53.770
The Nuckelavee’s abhorrence of the pungent
smell of burning seaweed, and perhaps his

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:59.009
perception of the act as an insult to his
ocean home, was said to be the cause of Mortasheen,

00:06:59.009 --> 00:07:04.539
an equine influenza that killed horses across
the kelp-burning islands.

00:07:04.539 --> 00:07:09.650
The Orkney kelp trade largely died out in
the 20th century, which corresponds with a

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:12.460
decline in belief in the Nuckelavee.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:17.039
Mortasheen was only one example of the real
devastation blamed on the monster.

00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:21.780
The Nuckelavee was thought to be responsible
for destroyed crops, dead animals, and disease

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:27.110
of all kinds which were said to be caused
by his control of mildew, salty air from the

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.259
sea, or the creature’s own terrible breath.

00:07:30.259 --> 00:07:35.120
Oftentimes we use monsters to explain the
unexplainable: drought, famine, and disease.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:41.200
And for the Nuckelavee, it reveals how Scotland
is intimately connected to the landscape and

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:42.539
local identity.

00:07:42.539 --> 00:07:47.849
True, not all cultures create a monster that
takes shape as a disgusting, skinless, ugly

00:07:47.849 --> 00:07:50.159
cousin of centaurs—but hey.

00:07:50.159 --> 00:08:02.249
Apparently only Scotland is that cool.

